WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.171
Zero: Hello, little Human.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.241
I am Zealously Empathetic
Robotic Organism.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.443
You may call me ZERO.

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.178
Zero: I will help you humans.

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:15.915
I read in my guide to
Earth that humans like ozone.

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:19.986
Ozone will protect you
from harmful UV rays.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:23.156
But I need to get
my tank of ozone.

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:24.391
[Zap]

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:26.793
Zero: Okay, now I am ready. [Pleasant music]

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.863
Get ready for my
protection, humans.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.030
Protect!

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:36.102
Marathon dude: [coughs]

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.205
Zero: Protect!!

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:43.743
Lady: [Coughs violently]

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:46.679
Zero: Feel the warmth of
my protection, humans.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:48.481
Protecting...

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:57.624
Little Boy: [crying]

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.531
Zero: Feel the warmth
of my protection!

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.499
Zero: PROTECT!

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:07.801
Scientist: [coughs]

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:08.868
Scientist: Aah!

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.403
Why did you just do that?

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.372
What did you just
spray me with?

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:15.575
Zero: It says in my guidebook
that the ozone layer protects

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.678
humans from UV rays.

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:21.181
I have sprayed
you with ozone.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:23.383
You are now protected, Human.

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.084
You are welcome.

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:26.686
Scientist: No,
man, you're wrong.

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:27.987
Well, sort of...

00:01:27.987 --> 00:01:30.356
I'm a research scientist,
I know this stuff.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.258
Here, come with me.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.262
Scientist: I think you're
a little bit confused about

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.965
ozone, so I'm going
to explain it to you.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:40.867
First, it's important
to know that ozone is a

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:45.238
chemical-specifically a gas-with a chemical structure of (O3).

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.973
Here, we're almost at my lab.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:50.510
Scientist: Come with me,
there's a special room

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.545
in the back that I
would like to show you.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.981
Scientist: We call this
our periodic table room;

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:56.115
here, have a look.

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:56.983
Zero: Oh, look!

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.519
There is iridium; that
is what the currency

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.454
on my planet is made of.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.190
Your money is
worthless, Human.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.660
Scientist: So, as I was
saying, ozone is (O3),

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.230
meaning it's composed
of 3 oxygen atoms.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.798
Zero: Contradiction, Human!

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:15.134
I thought oxygen atoms are
what you humans breathe.

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:16.536
Scientist: You're right,
but the difference between

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:19.973
the oxygen we breathe and
ozone is that oxygen gas is

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:23.376
the molecule (O2),
and ozone is (O3).

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:25.011
Because of this
difference, they each have

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.346
different properties.

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:29.916
Zero: Query, what are
the properties of ozone?

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.651
Scientist: Well first, you
need to know that there are

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.920
two different
types of ozone.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.555
Let's go outside
and I can show you.

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:41.661
Scientist: The first
kind of ozone is called

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.263
stratospheric ozone.

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:45.865
It exists in what is
called the ozone layer,

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:49.702
which is about 6 to 30 miles
above the surface of the earth.

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:52.605
The other kind of ozone is
called tropospheric ozone.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.507
It is located at the
earth's surface.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.245
Zero: Query, what else
makes stratospheric and

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.881
tropospheric
ozone different?

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:03.816
Scientist: Well for one,
how they are formed.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:06.753
I think we're going to need
a different perspective.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:07.520
Hmm...

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.289
Zero: I have a hypothesis.

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:11.424
I am equipped with a solution!

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.659
Hang on tightly.

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:20.633
Scientist: [with outrage] Hey!

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:22.168
Couldn't you have
given me a heads up?

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:23.202
Zero: Oopsies.

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:24.103
Scientist: Fine!

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.672
Where was I?

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:29.175
Zero: You were describing how
stratospheric ozone is formed.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.643
Scientist: Oh
yes, that's right.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:32.779
First of all, here's
the stratosphere.

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:36.149
So stratospheric ozone
is naturally formed when

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:40.119
ultraviolet rays interact
with oxygen gas, (O2),

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.521
in the stratosphere.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:44.924
The photons from the UV
rays break the (O2) apart into

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:47.627
two separate
single oxygen atoms.

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:51.097
Then an individual oxygen
atom bonds with an (O2),

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:54.033
to make (O3), or ozone.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:56.669
Scientist: Now will you
please take me back to earth?

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.641
Zero: Information
input computes.

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:03.743
Query, why does
stratospheric ozone

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.012
only form in the stratosphere?

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:06.980
Scientist: Good question.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.682
You asked me earlier what
the properties of ozone are.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.652
One property is that it
is unstable, much more so

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.621
than oxygen gas, or (O2).

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:16.589
Scientist: Because of
this, stratospheric ozone

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.825
cannot exist on the
earth's surface, where

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:21.394
atmospheric pressure and
temperature are so high.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:23.062
Scientist: These conditions
would cause the unstable

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:26.899
ozone to decay and revert
into oxygen gas, or (O2).

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:28.534
Scientist: But, higher
up in the atmosphere,

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.903
pressure and temperature
are much lower, making it

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.206
a better place for
stratospheric ozone.

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:36.376
Zero: Query-so what about
tropospheric ozone?

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:37.877
How does that form?

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:41.147
Scientist: So tropospheric
ozone is unlike stratospheric

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:43.549
ozone because it is not natural.

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.551
It is formed by
photochemical reactions

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:49.022
between air pollutants,
mainly VOC and NOx.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:49.789
Zero: Query!

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.958
What's all that
stuff about?

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:54.661
Scientist: Well, a photochemical
reaction is a reaction that

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:58.131
requires the absorption
of light energy to occur.

00:04:58.131 --> 00:05:01.701
Scientist: VOC stands for
Volatile Organic Compounds.

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:04.370
These are usually in
the form of gases.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.839
Scientist: NOx stands for
Nitrogen Oxides, such as

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:11.244
(NO), nitrogen oxide, and
(NO2), nitrogen dioxide.

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:14.981
Both VOC and NOx are
types of air pollutants.

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.316
Zero: Pollutant does
not sound very good.

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:20.053
Query, where do these
pollutants come from?

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.487
Scientist: You're
right, it isn't good.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:22.955
Here, follow me.

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:29.462
Scientist: The simple
answer is that these

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:31.631
pollutants come from humans.

00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:34.200
Specifically, cars,
factories, and more

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.003
release VOCs and
NOx into the air.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.504
Scientist: Then these
pollutants react to

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.640
form tropospheric ozone.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.609
Zero: Query, why doesn't
tropospheric ozone revert to

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:46.813
oxygen gas, just like
stratospheric ozone would if

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:48.581
it were in the troposphere?

00:05:48.581 --> 00:05:49.782
Scientist: Well, it does.

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:52.418
The thing is that humans
produce so many pollutants, that

00:05:52.418 --> 00:05:55.388
they are constantly refilling
the troposphere with ozone.

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:57.490
Scientist: [Coughs] But
let's get out of here;

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.792
this place isn't
good for my health.

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:03.863
Zero: Query, what do you mean

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:06.466
"isn't good for
your health," Human?

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:08.768
Scientist: Well, because the
tropospheric ozone is made from

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:11.270
pollutants, it is
a pollutant itself,

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.339
which makes it
harmful to humans.

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.241
Scientist: It's also
a greenhouse gas that

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:17.110
contributes to
global warming.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.412
Zero: This is much
information to compute.

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:21.781
Let me check if I
am understanding.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:24.450
Scientist: Go ahead.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.820
Zero: Stratospheric ozone forms naturally in the ozone layer

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.555
up in the stratosphere.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:32.692
Scientist: Yes, 6 to 30
miles above the earth.

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:35.261
Zero: And tropospheric
ozone forms on earth's

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:38.698
surface from the reaction
of air pollutants.

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:42.034
In addition, tropospheric
ozone is harmful to human

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:44.504
health and is a
greenhouse gas.

00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:46.005
Scientist: Yup,
that's correct.

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.841
Zero: Query, if
tropospheric ozone is bad,

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:51.477
is stratospheric
ozone also bad?

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:52.678
Scientist: Actually no.

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:53.746
This goes back to
when you were first

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:55.581
spraying people with ozone.

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:57.583
You were half right when
you said that ozone protects

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.218
people from UV rays.

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:00.820
Zero: Hypothesis validated!

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.321
I am helpful!

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:04.323
Zealous Empathy wins again.

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:05.792
Scientist: Only
stratospheric ozone

00:07:05.792 --> 00:07:07.894
protects people from UV rays.

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:10.463
Would you mind taking
us back to space?

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:16.102
Scientist: Thanks.

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:18.971
Scientist: So as I was saying,
ozone becomes a sort of

00:07:18.971 --> 00:07:24.043
natural shield that absorbs 93
to 99% of all harmful UV rays,

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:26.746
which can cause skin cancer
and cataracts in people.

00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:29.482
Scientist: But this only
happens in the stratosphere.

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:31.617
You, on the other hand,
were spraying people with

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:35.221
tropospheric ozone because
you were on the earth's surface.

00:07:35.221 --> 00:07:37.557
Zero: Conundrum, isn't
tropospheric ozone

00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:39.325
bad for human health?

00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:41.961
[Gasp] Did I hurt
those humans?

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:44.397
Scientist: Well, it
definitely wasn't good for them.

00:07:44.397 --> 00:07:46.098
However, I don't think you
sprayed them with enough

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:48.768
ozone to cause
any real problems.

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:52.171
Zero: If I felt
emotions-it would be relief.

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:55.007
Query, if I had sprayed
them with more ozone, then

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:57.777
what would have been
the negative effects?

00:07:57.777 --> 00:07:59.812
Scientist: Well, there
are actually a lot.

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:00.847
Let's get back to
the earth so that

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:02.348
I can show you some examples.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:08.387
Scientist: Thanks.

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:11.324
So when a human breathes
in tropospheric ozone,

00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:14.227
the ozone damages the
respiratory tract, leading to

00:08:14.227 --> 00:08:17.230
respiratory symptoms,
reduced lung function, and

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:18.764
airway inflammation.

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:22.301
Scientist: In severe cases,
ozone can even cause death.

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:24.570
Zero: Such a sad
tragedy for inferior

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.806
carbon-based life forms.

00:08:26.806 --> 00:08:29.842
Zero: Human, list the
respiratory symptoms!

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:32.778
Scientist: The main respiratory
symptoms include coughing,

00:08:32.778 --> 00:08:36.649
throat irritation, pain,
burning, discomfort or tightness

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:40.453
in the chest, wheezing,
or shortness of breath.

00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:45.625
Zero: Wow, ZERO is happy to
have unfeeling automaton organs.

00:08:45.625 --> 00:08:46.993
Scientist: Yes,
you should be.

00:08:46.993 --> 00:08:49.662
And if a person breathes in
too much ozone or a lot over

00:08:49.662 --> 00:08:52.732
a long period of time, then
the effects are greater, and

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:54.400
can lead to death.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:57.403
Zero: If I were capable of
expressing colorful metaphor,

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:59.739
I would be expressing one now.

00:08:59.739 --> 00:09:01.574
Zero: I am glad you
stopped me from spraying

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.342
more people with ozone.

00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:07.313
My trip to Hollywood, Florida
might have been cancelled.

00:09:07.313 --> 00:09:09.515
Zero: So we have learned
about stratospheric and

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:13.552
tropospheric ozone, how they
form, where they exist and why,

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:16.589
and health effects of
both on puny humans.

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:18.391
Do I require more data?

00:09:18.391 --> 00:09:20.760
Scientist: Well, there is one
more present day matter that

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:24.263
I wanted to discuss with
you-ozone depletion.

00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:25.665
Zero: Ozone depletion?

00:09:25.665 --> 00:09:27.199
Explain, Human.

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:29.468
Scientist: Let's get back
to my lab so I can show you.

00:09:31.604 --> 00:09:33.639
Scientist: So remember how
the ozone layer protects

00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:35.474
Earth from UV rays?

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:36.742
Zero: Affirmative.

00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:39.178
Scientist: Well, ozone
depletion is a decrease in

00:09:39.178 --> 00:09:41.447
the amount of ozone
in the ozone layer.

00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:45.017
This allows more harmful
UV rays to reach earth.

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:46.819
Zero: But this
increases human risk to

00:09:46.819 --> 00:09:48.788
skin cancer and cataracts!

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:51.757
Query, why is the
ozone layer depleting?

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:54.226
Scientist: Starting in the
1920s, people began using

00:09:54.226 --> 00:09:57.730
chlorofluorocarbon, or
CFC, in refrigerators and

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.231
aerosol sprays.

00:09:59.231 --> 00:10:03.669
CFC is a VOC, made of
carbon, chlorine, and fluorine.

00:10:03.669 --> 00:10:05.905
Scientist: CFC compounds
don't react with anything in

00:10:05.905 --> 00:10:08.874
the lower atmosphere so they
go into the upper atmosphere

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:12.144
where UV rays break them
apart into individual atoms.

00:10:12.144 --> 00:10:15.247
Scientist: Then, some of
chlorine atoms STEAL oxygen

00:10:15.247 --> 00:10:17.883
atoms from the
unstable ozone molecules,

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:20.553
turning the ozone
back into oxygen.

00:10:20.553 --> 00:10:24.090
This entire process is
known as ozone depletion.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:27.093
Zero: Why do you say only
some chlorine atoms?

00:10:27.093 --> 00:10:29.028
Scientist: I think
we'll need a visual.

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:30.563
Let's go back to space.

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:36.068
Scientist: As I mentioned
previously, CFCs go into

00:10:36.068 --> 00:10:37.837
the atmosphere, where
the chlorines are

00:10:37.837 --> 00:10:39.672
photochemically separated.

00:10:39.672 --> 00:10:43.142
Scientist: Then, most chlorine
atoms bond with other chemicals.

00:10:43.142 --> 00:10:45.845
These new stable molecules
follow air currents to

00:10:45.845 --> 00:10:47.613
different places
in the stratosphere.

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:49.915
Scientist: When these compounds
eventually end up at the poles,

00:10:49.915 --> 00:10:53.519
polar vortexes and stratospheric
clouds separate the chlorines,

00:10:53.519 --> 00:10:56.222
then the chlorine atoms bond
with the oxygen atoms from

00:10:56.222 --> 00:11:00.026
ozone in the ozone layer,
thereby depleting the ozone.

00:11:00.026 --> 00:11:01.594
Scientist: This creates
ozone holes above the

00:11:01.594 --> 00:11:03.295
North and South Poles.

00:11:03.295 --> 00:11:04.897
Zero: That sounds like peril.

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:07.833
I guess it is good that
nobody lives on those Poles...

00:11:07.833 --> 00:11:10.503
except all those tiny
black and white humans with

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:12.038
beaks I see down there.

00:11:12.038 --> 00:11:13.339
Zero: Hello,
little humans.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:15.741
They are so funny.

00:11:15.741 --> 00:11:16.776
Scientist: That's true.

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:19.645
But Australia is very
close to the large ozone

00:11:19.645 --> 00:11:21.580
hole over Antarctica.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.416
Scientist: Australians suffer
from the highest rates of

00:11:23.416 --> 00:11:26.118
skin cancer, with
about 1,200 Australians

00:11:26.118 --> 00:11:28.587
dying every year
from the disease.

00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:30.790
Zero: Zealous
Empathy activated!

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:34.994
So to summarize, ozone
depletion happens everywhere,

00:11:34.994 --> 00:11:37.763
even over London, Kentucky.

00:11:37.763 --> 00:11:40.933
Zero: But ozone holes are
only occurring over the Poles.

00:11:40.933 --> 00:11:44.303
Both are mainly caused
by CFC emissions.

00:11:44.303 --> 00:11:46.038
Scientist: Yes, that's right.

00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:48.274
Zero: Now that we know
about this, humans have

